window.FM_DATA["core"] = {"id":"core","title":"Core Utilities","entries":[{"id":"singleton","name":"Singleton<T>","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/Singleton/Singleton.cs","src_lines":[11,76],"src_base":"MonoBehaviour where T : Singleton<T>","src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Generic MonoBehaviour singleton. Inherit as: public class GameManager : Singleton<GameManager>  The constraint is `where T : Singleton<T>` (the curiously recurring pattern), NOT `where T : MonoBehaviour`. With the looser constraint, `class Foo : Singleton<Bar>` compiled happily and then `this as T` silently evaluated to null at runtime, leaving Instance permanently empty with nothing to explain why. This way that mistake will not compile.","src_serialized":[{"name":"persistAcrossScenes","type":"bool","default":"false","tooltip":"If true, this object survives scene loads (DontDestroyOnLoad).","header":null,"range":null,"order":0,"comment":null}],"src_members":[{"id":"Instance","sig":"public static T Instance","kind":"property","name":"Instance","static":true,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":[],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Abstract generic base giving a MonoBehaviour a static Instance, a duplicate guard, and optional DontDestroyOnLoad.","problem":"Some objects genuinely should exist exactly once — the audio manager, the input manager, the tween runner — and every script that needs one should be able to say so without a serialized reference dragged in per scene. Singleton<T> supplies the three pieces that pattern always needs: a static Instance that finds or creates the object on first access, an Awake that destroys any second copy that sneaks into a scene, and a serialized opt-in for surviving scene loads. Inherit as `public class GameManager : Singleton<GameManager>` and the whole thing is 45 lines. It is convenient and it hides real lifecycle sharp edges — read the gotchas before you lean on it.","params":[{"field":"persistAcrossScenes","why":"When true, Awake calls DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject) so this object and its children survive every subsequent scene load.","tuning":"True for genuinely global services (audio, input, save data); false for managers that are meaningfully per-scene, or you end up with a stale manager holding references to objects from a scene that no longer exists. Note that an auto-created instance always gets false, because there is no Inspector to set it on."}],"workedExample":{"na":"Singleton<T> has no numeric behaviour to trace — it is a lifetime and ownership rule, not a calculation. Its interesting cases are all ordering ones (who registers first, what happens to the duplicate, what Instance returns during shutdown), and those are covered member by member in the gotchas below."},"useCases":["A game or round manager that scene code and UI both need to reach without a wiring step.","Input managers: LegacyInputManager and NewInputSystemManager both derive from this so any script can poll the active scheme.","Services that should materialise on first use rather than be placed by hand — TweenRunner is auto-created this way so a tween call from any script just works.","A settings or save manager that must outlive scene loads, using persistAcrossScenes.","An update aggregator such as UpdateManager, where the whole point is one Update loop rather than hundreds."],"usage":{"code":"public class GameManager : Singleton<GameManager>\n{\n    protected override void Awake()\n    {\n        base.Awake();   // registers the instance, or destroys this duplicate\n        // your own setup here\n    }\n}\n// elsewhere:  GameManager.Instance.StartRound();","codeNote":"Instance is a public static T property on the base class (it does not appear in the member table below — an extractor gap, not a missing API). Its getter returns null while quitting, otherwise returns the cached instance, otherwise FindObjectOfType<T>(), otherwise creates a new GameObject named after T and AddComponents T onto it."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Instance silently manufactures a GameObject when none exists","body":"If the cached instance is null and FindObjectOfType finds nothing, the getter does `new GameObject(typeof(T).Name)` and AddComponent<T>() — with no log line. That object gets persistAcrossScenes = false (there is no Inspector to say otherwise), so it dies at the next scene load and the following access quietly creates another one. Several toolkit managers (FadeManager, ShaderTransitionManager, SettingsManager) carry explicit lazy-init code precisely because of this behaviour."},{"title":"FindObjectOfType does not see inactive objects","body":"A singleton you placed in the scene but left disabled is invisible to the getter, so touching Instance creates a second one. Enable the original later and its Awake sees instance != this — and destroys it. Never ship a disabled singleton and expect Instance to find it."},{"title":"The duplicate guard destroys the whole GameObject","body":"Awake's losing branch is `Destroy(gameObject)`, not Destroy(this). Everything else on that object, and every child of it, goes with the duplicate component. Put a singleton on its own dedicated GameObject, never bolted onto a character or a UI root."},{"title":"A subclass that forgets base.Awake() silently breaks the pattern","body":"Awake is protected virtual. Declaring `private void Awake()` in your subclass hides it rather than overriding it, so nothing registers the instance, the duplicate guard never runs and persistAcrossScenes is ignored — with no compiler error. Always `protected override void Awake()` and call base.Awake() first. The same applies to OnApplicationQuit."},{"title":"The shutdown flag resets at the start of every play session","body":"Unity fires OnApplicationQuit when you leave PLAY MODE too, not only when a build exits. With Domain Reload disabled the static latched true at the end of one session and Instance returned null from the first frame of the NEXT one. Both the flag and the cached instance are now cleared by a RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod, which runs whether or not domain reload is on."},{"title":"The constraint is now `where T : Singleton<T>`","body":"With the looser MonoBehaviour constraint, `class Foo : Singleton<Bar>` compiled and then `this as T` silently evaluated to null, leaving Instance permanently empty with nothing to explain it. That mistake no longer compiles. All 17 subclasses in the toolkit already used the correct form."},{"title":"DontDestroyOnLoad only works on a root GameObject","body":"persistAcrossScenes calls DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject), which Unity only honours for objects at the root of the hierarchy. A singleton nested under a scene-organising parent will still be destroyed on load, and the next Instance access will auto-create a replacement. Keep persistent singletons unparented."}],"seeAlso":["TweenRunner","SettingsManager","UpdateManager"],"src_uses":["ModalDialog","ToastNotification"],"src_usedBy":["AsyncSceneLoader","BattleState","FadeManager","FloatingCombatTextSpawner","FrameBudgetProcessor","IdleAttractManager","LegacyInputManager","ModalDialog"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"object-pool","name":"ObjectPool","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/ObjectPool/ObjectPool.cs","src_lines":[8,164],"src_base":"MonoBehaviour","src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{"addComponentMenu":"Toolkit/Object Pool"},"src_typeComment":"Attach to an empty GameObject to pool a single prefab type, cuts down on Instantiate/Destroy GC spikes.","src_serialized":[{"name":"prefab","type":"GameObject","default":null,"tooltip":"The prefab this pool will spawn and reuse.","header":null,"range":null,"order":0,"comment":null},{"name":"prewarmCount","type":"int","default":"10","tooltip":"How many instances to create upfront so the first spawns don't hitch.","header":null,"range":null,"order":1,"comment":null},{"name":"allowGrowth","type":"bool","default":"true","tooltip":"If true, requesting more objects than exist will grow the pool instead of returning null.","header":null,"range":null,"order":2,"comment":null},{"name":"poolParent","type":"Transform","default":null,"tooltip":"Parent transform inactive pooled objects are stored under, keeps the Hierarchy tidy. Defaults to this object.","header":null,"range":null,"order":3,"comment":null}],"src_members":[{"id":"Initialize(GameObject, int, bool, Transform)","sig":"public void Initialize(GameObject newPrefab, int newPrewarmCount = -1, bool newAllowGrowth = true, Transform newPoolParent = null)","kind":"method","name":"Initialize","static":false,"comment":"Configures this pool from code — for a component that creates its own ObjectPool at runtime (e.g. via gameObject.AddComponent&lt;ObjectPool&gt;()) instead of pre-wiring one in the Inspector. Safe to call regardless of whether Awake has already run (Awake skips prewarming when prefab is still null). newPrewarmCount defaults to -1 meaning \"leave whatever is already configured\", NOT 0. It used to default to 0, so calling Initialize(prefab) to set just the prefab silently overwrote the Inspector's prewarm count with zero and removed the prewarming the field was asking for."},{"id":"Get(Vector3, Quaternion)","sig":"public GameObject Get(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)","kind":"method","name":"Get","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Get()","sig":"public GameObject Get() => Get(poolParent != null ? poolParent.position : transform.position,","kind":"method","name":"Get","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Release(GameObject)","sig":"public void Release(GameObject instance)","kind":"method","name":"Release","static":false,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo","GameplayArena_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"One-prefab MonoBehaviour pool: prewarms instances, hands them out with Get(), takes them back with Release().","problem":"Instantiate and Destroy are the two most expensive routine calls in a Unity game: Instantiate allocates and deserialises a whole object graph, and Destroy hands the corpse to the GC, which eventually stops the world to collect it. Anything spawned in bursts — bullets, impact effects, damage numbers, enemy waves — turns that into a visible hitch. ObjectPool trades memory for time: it creates N copies up front, deactivates them, and then recycling is just SetActive plus a position write. Attach it to an empty GameObject per prefab type, or build one from code with Initialize().","params":[{"field":"prefab","why":"The single prefab this pool spawns and reuses. One pool serves exactly one prefab; a second prefab needs a second ObjectPool component.","tuning":"It does not need to be a project asset — a deactivated scene object works, which is how FlyToUIEffect pools a UI icon template."},{"field":"prewarmCount","why":"How many instances are Instantiated during Awake so the first spawns do not hitch. These are created deactivated under poolParent.","tuning":"Set it to your realistic peak concurrency, not your total spawn count — a pool that serves 500 bullets over a level but never more than 20 at once only needs 20. Too high just costs scene-load time and memory."},{"field":"allowGrowth","why":"What happens when Get() is called and the stack is empty: true Instantiates another (the pool grows permanently), false returns null.","tuning":"Leave true unless you deliberately want a hard cap on concurrent objects — a bullet-hell limiter, or an FX budget. With false, every call site must handle null."},{"field":"poolParent","why":"The Transform inactive instances are stored under, purely to keep the Hierarchy tidy. Defaults to this pool's own transform in Awake when left empty.","tuning":"Keep whatever you point it at at identity scale: instances are parented to it and never unparented, so a scaled poolParent visually rescales every pooled object."}],"workedExample":{"setup":"The synchronous half of FlyToUIEffectSelfTest: an ObjectPool added at runtime with AddComponent<ObjectPool>() and then configured with Initialize(prefab, newPrewarmCount: 2, newAllowGrowth: true, newPoolParent: poolGO.transform).","inputs":[{"name":"prewarmCount","value":"2"},{"name":"allowGrowth","value":"true"}],"steps":["AddComponent runs Awake immediately, while prefab is still null. The `if (prefab != null)` guard skips Prewarm rather than calling Instantiate(null, ...), and poolParent falls back to the pool's own transform. Nothing has been instantiated yet.","Initialize() assigns the prefab and calls Prewarm(), which pushes prewarmCount = 2 deactivated instances onto the stack. The self-test asserts poolGO.transform.childCount == 2.","a = ObjectPool.Get(Vector3.zero, Quaternion.identity) pops the top of the stack (call it I2), positions it and activates it. Stack depth 2 -> 1; total Instantiate calls so far: 2.","ObjectPool.Release(a) deactivates I2, reparents it to poolParent and pushes it back. Stack depth 1 -> 2, with I2 on top.","b = ObjectPool.Get(...) pops the top again, which is the very instance just released, so b is reference-equal to a. The self-test asserts a == b: three Get calls have been served by two Instantiate calls.","c = ObjectPool.Get(...) while b is still live pops the other prewarmed instance I1, so c != b and the stack is now empty. A fourth concurrent Get() would find nothing and, because allowGrowth is true, Instantiate a third object — permanently raising the pool's size to the new high-water mark."],"result":"Two Instantiate calls serve three Get() calls, and the pool only ever grows when more objects are live simultaneously than have ever been live before. Reuse is exact object identity, not a copy: a == b.","verifiedBy":"selftest:Assets/UnityTools/Toolkit/UI/FlyToUI/Editor/FlyToUIEffectSelfTest.cs"},"useCases":["Projectiles — bullets, arrows, grenades — where fire rate makes Instantiate/Destroy churn unavoidable otherwise.","Impact and muzzle effects, paired with PoolableObject's autoReleaseDelay so they return themselves with no caller bookkeeping.","Floating combat text and other UI bursts: FloatingCombatTextSpawner and FlyToUIEffect both AddComponent an ObjectPool at runtime and configure it via Initialize().","Enemy waves in a tower-defence or survival loop, where the same handful of prefabs cycle continuously.","Any list of UI rows that is rebuilt as the player scrolls or filters."],"usage":{"code":"[SerializeField] private ObjectPool bulletPool;\n\nGameObject bullet = bulletPool.Get(muzzle.position, muzzle.rotation);\nif (bullet == null) return;      // only possible when allowGrowth is false\n// ...later, instead of Destroy(bullet):\nbulletPool.Release(bullet);","codeNote":"Release is the counterpart to Get — calling Destroy on a pooled instance instead leaks it out of the pool permanently and the pool will just Instantiate a replacement."},"apiNotes":{"Initialize(GameObject, int, bool, Transform)":"Configures the pool from code, for components that create their own pool at runtime instead of having one wired in the Inspector. It is safe to call after Awake has already run, because Awake skips prewarming when prefab is null. It both assigns all four settings and prewarms in one call — but note its newPrewarmCount default is 0, not the serialized default of 10.","Get(Vector3, Quaternion)":"Pops an instance (or Instantiates one if the stack is empty and allowGrowth is true), applies the position and rotation with SetPositionAndRotation, activates it, and calls NotifySpawned() on its PoolableObject if it has one. Returns null when the stack is empty and allowGrowth is false. It does not reparent, so the instance stays a child of poolParent while live.","Get()":"Convenience overload defined as Get(poolParent.position, poolParent.rotation) — it spawns AT the pool's parent, which is right for UI under a canvas rect and almost never right for a world object. It also dereferences poolParent, which is only assigned by Awake or Initialize.","Release(GameObject)":"Returns an instance: rejects the call outright if the instance has a PoolableObject already flagged IsPooled, then deactivates, reparents to poolParent, calls NotifyDespawned() and pushes it back on the stack. It does not verify the instance came from this pool, and prefabs without a PoolableObject get no double-release protection at all."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Double-release corrupts the pool on prefabs without PoolableObject","body":"Release's only guard is `if (poolable != null && poolable.IsPooled) return;`. On a plain prefab there is no PoolableObject, so two systems both reacting to the same 'death' and both calling Release push the same GameObject onto the stack twice — and two later Get() calls then hand out the same live object to two owners. The source comment flags this explicitly. Add a PoolableObject to any prefab that more than one system can retire."},{"title":"Changing the prefab rebuilds the pool","body":"A private latch meant a second Initialize with a different prefab swapped the field but kept the OLD prefab's instances, so the pool went on handing out the previous prefab until the stack drained — a swap that looked like it worked and then quietly did the wrong thing. Initialize now detects a prefab change, destroys the pooled instances and prewarms again."},{"title":"Initialize leaves prewarmCount alone unless you pass one","body":"The parameter defaulted to 0, so calling Initialize(prefab) to set just the prefab overwrote the Inspector's count with zero and removed the prewarming that field was asking for. It now defaults to -1, meaning \"keep what is configured\"."},{"title":"allowGrowth = false makes Get() return null","body":"It does not throw, and it does not recycle the oldest live object — it just returns null. Every call site needs a null check; the Core demo runner does `if (instance != null) spawnCount++`. Silent no-spawns are the usual symptom of a too-small pool with growth disabled."},{"title":"The prefab's scale is restored on the way out, and a scaled pool parent is reported","body":"Instances stay parented on purpose — pooled UI parents its pool to a Canvas, and a RectTransform outside a Canvas does not render at all, so unparenting would break every UI pool to fix a transform problem. Get now restores the prefab's own localScale, and a pool parent with a non-identity world scale logs a warning once, since that is a setup mistake rather than something to silently compensate for."},{"title":"A recycled object keeps all its old state","body":"The entire reset is SetActive(false) then SetActive(true) plus a position write. Rigidbody velocity, health, animator parameters, particle state, material property overrides, trail renderer history and any field you mutated all survive into the next spawn. Put the reset in a PoolableObject.OnSpawned handler, which fires from Get() after the object is activated."}],"seeAlso":["PoolableObject","FlyToUIEffect","FloatingCombatTextSpawner"],"src_uses":["FloatingCombatTextSpawner","FlyToUIEffect","PoolableObject"],"src_usedBy":["FloatingCombatTextSpawner","FlyToUIEffect","PoolableObject","ProjectileMover"],"src_extensionPoints":[],"isSystem":true,"systemOf":"ObjectPool","systemPrimaryId":"object-pool","systemSections":[{"title":"Supporting scripts","entries":[{"id":"poolable-object","name":"PoolableObject","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/ObjectPool/PoolableObject.cs","src_lines":[9,74],"src_base":"MonoBehaviour","src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{"addComponentMenu":"Toolkit/Poolable Object"},"src_typeComment":"Optional companion to ObjectPool: gives pooled objects lifecycle events and a self-release timer. Adding this is optional — ObjectPool works fine on plain prefabs without it.","src_serialized":[{"name":"autoReleaseDelay","type":"float","default":"0f","tooltip":"If greater than 0, this object automatically releases itself back to its pool after this many seconds. 0 = stays until manually released.","header":null,"range":null,"order":0,"comment":null}],"src_members":[{"id":"OnSpawned","sig":"public event Action OnSpawned","kind":"event","name":"OnSpawned","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"OnDespawned","sig":"public event Action OnDespawned","kind":"event","name":"OnDespawned","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"SourcePool","sig":"public ObjectPool SourcePool { get; set; }","kind":"property","name":"SourcePool","static":false,"comment":"Assigned by ObjectPool when this instance is created; lets the object release itself without holding a manual reference."},{"id":"IsPooled","sig":"public bool IsPooled { get; private set; } = true","kind":"property","name":"IsPooled","static":false,"comment":"True while this instance is sitting inactive in its pool. ObjectPool checks this to reject a double-release (e.g. two different scripts both calling Release() on the same object), which would otherwise push the same instance into the pool twice and let two later Get() calls hand out the same live object. Starts TRUE. A prewarmed instance has never been spawned, so NotifySpawned has never run; with a default of false the double-release guard in ObjectPool.Release did not recognise those instances as already pooled, and releasing one pushed it onto the stack a second time - after which two Get() calls could hand out the same live object."},{"id":"PrefabLocalScale","sig":"public Vector3 PrefabLocalScale { get; set; } = Vector3.one","kind":"property","name":"PrefabLocalScale","static":false,"comment":"The prefab's own localScale, captured when the pool creates this instance. Parenting applies the parent's scale, so the pool restores this on the way out."},{"id":"NotifySpawned()","sig":"public void NotifySpawned()","kind":"method","name":"NotifySpawned","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"NotifyDespawned()","sig":"public void NotifyDespawned()","kind":"method","name":"NotifyDespawned","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Release()","sig":"public void Release()","kind":"method","name":"Release","static":false,"comment":"Manual release call, e.g. an enemy calling this on death instead of Destroy(gameObject)."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo","GameplayArena_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Optional companion component giving pooled prefabs spawn/despawn events, a self-release timer, and a double-release guard.","problem":"A pooled object is reused, not rebuilt, so two things are missing compared with Instantiate/Destroy: there is no constructor moment to reset state in, and there is no Destroy call to hang cleanup off. PoolableObject supplies both as OnSpawned/OnDespawned events, adds an optional self-release countdown for fire-and-forget effects, and carries a back-reference to its pool so the object can retire itself without anyone holding a pool reference. It is entirely optional — ObjectPool works on plain prefabs — but its IsPooled flag is also the only thing standing between you and a double-release bug.","params":[{"field":"autoReleaseDelay","why":"Seconds after spawning before the object returns itself to its pool. 0 disables the timer and the object stays out until something calls Release().","tuning":"Set it slightly longer than the visual it drives — a particle system's total duration including lifetime, or an animation clip's length. Too short and the effect pops out mid-play; too long and you need a bigger pool for the same spawn rate."}],"workedExample":{"setup":"A muzzle-flash prefab carrying PoolableObject with autoReleaseDelay = 0.25, spawned from an ObjectPool in a game running at 60 fps.","inputs":[{"name":"autoReleaseDelay","value":"0.25"},{"name":"frame step","value":"1/60 s"}],"steps":["ObjectPool.Get() activates the instance and calls NotifySpawned(), which sets IsPooled = false, and because autoReleaseDelay > 0 sets releaseTimer = 0.25 and countingDown = true, then fires OnSpawned.","Update subtracts Time.deltaTime each frame while countingDown. After 15 frames it has subtracted 15 x (1/60) = 0.25, so releaseTimer reaches 0. (Real deltaTime is never exactly 1/60, so in practice it fires on the first frame at or past 0.25 s of accumulated scaled time.)","releaseTimer <= 0, so countingDown is cleared and Release() runs.","Release() finds SourcePool non-null — ObjectPool assigned it in CreateInstance — and calls SourcePool.Release(gameObject), which deactivates the instance, reparents it to poolParent, calls NotifyDespawned() (IsPooled = true, OnDespawned fires) and pushes it back on the stack.","Because the countdown uses Time.deltaTime, it is scaled time: at Time.timeScale = 0.5 the same 0.25 s of game time takes 30 frames and 0.5 s of wall clock, and at timeScale 0 it never completes."],"result":"The flash returns itself to the pool 0.25 s of scaled time after spawning, with no caller bookkeeping and no Destroy — roughly 15 frames at 60 fps, or 0.5 s of wall clock under half-speed slow motion.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Timed visual effects — muzzle flashes, impact sparks, dust puffs — that should disappear on their own via autoReleaseDelay.","Resetting reused state: an OnSpawned handler that restores health, zeroes Rigidbody velocity and clears a TrailRenderer.","An enemy or projectile that retires itself on death by calling Release() instead of Destroy(gameObject), with no reference to the pool.","Any prefab that two different systems could retire (a bullet that both hits a wall and times out), where the IsPooled guard prevents a double-release."],"usage":{"code":"private void Awake()\n{\n    var poolable = GetComponent<PoolableObject>();\n    poolable.OnSpawned   += () => { health = maxHealth; body.velocity = Vector3.zero; };\n    poolable.OnDespawned += () => trail.Clear();\n}\n\nprivate void Die() => GetComponent<PoolableObject>().Release();","codeNote":"Subscribe once, in Awake — Awake runs at Instantiate time (i.e. during prewarm), not on every spawn, so the handlers are attached exactly once for the life of the instance."},"apiNotes":{"OnSpawned":"Raised at the end of NotifySpawned(), which ObjectPool.Get() calls after the GameObject has already been activated and positioned. This is the reset hook that replaces a constructor for pooled objects.","OnDespawned":"Raised at the end of NotifyDespawned(), which ObjectPool.Release() calls after deactivating and reparenting the instance. Use it for cleanup that must not wait for the next spawn — clearing a trail, stopping a looping sound.","SourcePool":"Set by ObjectPool.CreateInstance() on every instance it makes, so the object always knows where to go home. It is a plain public setter with no validation, which also lets you retarget an instance to a different pool — and lets a mis-wired object have no pool at all.","IsPooled":"True only while the instance is sitting inactive in its pool. ObjectPool.Release() reads it to reject a double-release, which would otherwise push the same instance onto the stack twice and let two later Get() calls hand out the same live object. Note it is false on a prewarmed instance that has never been spawned.","NotifySpawned()":"Called by ObjectPool, not by you: clears IsPooled, arms the auto-release countdown if autoReleaseDelay > 0, then fires OnSpawned. Calling it yourself desynchronises IsPooled from whether the instance is actually on the pool's stack.","NotifyDespawned()":"Called by ObjectPool, not by you: sets IsPooled, cancels any countdown in progress, then fires OnDespawned.","Release()":"The one method meant for your own code. Routes to SourcePool.Release(gameObject) when a pool is known, and falls back to Destroy(gameObject) when SourcePool is null — so it is always a safe replacement for Destroy, even on an instance that was never pooled."},"gotchas":[{"title":"IsPooled starts true, so prewarmed instances are protected","body":"It defaulted to false, and a prewarmed instance has never been spawned so NotifySpawned had never run — which meant the double-release guard did not recognise those instances as already pooled, and releasing one pushed it onto the stack twice. Two later Get calls could then hand out the same live object. It now starts true."},{"title":"Release() destroys the object when SourcePool is null","body":"That fallback is deliberate (it makes Release a drop-in for Destroy), but it means a prefab that was Instantiated directly rather than obtained from a pool silently goes back to Instantiate/Destroy churn, with no warning in the console. If you see GC spikes despite pooling, check that the instances really came from ObjectPool.Get()."},{"title":"The auto-release timer is scaled time and only runs while active","body":"The countdown subtracts Time.deltaTime from Update, so Time.timeScale = 0 freezes it indefinitely — a UI effect spawned over a paused menu will hang forever. Update also does not run on a deactivated GameObject, which is exactly why a manual Release mid-countdown is safe (NotifyDespawned clears the flag anyway)."},{"title":"autoReleaseDelay is sampled at spawn, not read continuously","body":"NotifySpawned copies it into releaseTimer once. Changing the field on a live instance does not shorten or extend the countdown already in flight; it takes effect from the next spawn."},{"title":"Event handlers accumulate if you subscribe on spawn","body":"There is no clear or unsubscribe step anywhere in the lifecycle — an instance that is spawned and released 200 times keeps whatever handlers it has. Subscribing to OnSpawned from inside an OnSpawned handler, or from a script that runs on every spawn, stacks duplicates that all fire. Subscribe in Awake."}],"seeAlso":["ObjectPool"],"src_uses":["ObjectPool"],"src_usedBy":["ObjectPool","ProjectileMover"],"src_extensionPoints":[]}]}]},{"id":"state-machine","name":"StateMachine","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/StateMachine/StateMachine.cs","src_lines":[9,69],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Plain C# state machine, not a MonoBehaviour — call Tick() yourself from an owning MonoBehaviour's Update(). Kept framework-free on purpose: no reflection, no attribute-based transitions, so it's easy to trace and edit.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"CurrentState","sig":"public IState CurrentState { get; private set; }","kind":"property","name":"CurrentState","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"SetState(IState)","sig":"public void SetState(IState newState)","kind":"method","name":"SetState","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Tick()","sig":"public void Tick()","kind":"method","name":"Tick","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"IsInState<T>()","sig":"public bool IsInState<T>() where T : IState => CurrentState is T","kind":"method","name":"IsInState","static":false,"comment":"Sugar for AddTransition conditions and outside checks alike, e.g. AddTransition(chase, attack, () => stateMachine.IsInState<ChaseState>() && inRange)."},{"id":"AddTransition(IState, IState, Func<bool>)","sig":"public void AddTransition(IState from, IState to, Func<bool> condition)","kind":"method","name":"AddTransition","static":false,"comment":"Optional helper: register \"if condition true, switch to this state\" checked every Tick before the state runs. Skip this entirely and call SetState() manually from your own game logic if you'd rather not use rule-based transitions."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Plain C# finite state machine over IState, with optional condition-based transitions; you pump Tick() yourself.","problem":"Behaviour written as an enum plus a switch plus a handful of bools stops being readable at about the third mode, mostly because there is nowhere to put the work that has to happen when a mode starts and ends. StateMachine gives each mode a class (see IState) and centralises the switching: SetState runs Exit on the old and Enter on the new, and the optional transition table turns 'if X then go to Y' into data you register once rather than branching scattered through Update. It is intentionally tiny and framework-free — no reflection, no attributes, no MonoBehaviour — so the whole flow is traceable by reading 50 lines.","workedExample":{"setup":"StateMachineDemoRunner: two states whose Tick() accumulates Time.deltaTime into a public Timer that Enter() zeroes, with AddTransition(idle, active, () => idle.Timer >= 2f) and the mirror rule, running at a steady 60 fps (deltaTime = 1/60 s).","inputs":[{"name":"deltaTime","value":"1/60 s"},{"name":"transition threshold","value":"2f seconds"}],"steps":["Start() calls machine.SetState(idle). CurrentState was null so no Exit() runs; CurrentState becomes idle and idle.Enter() sets Timer = 0.","Each Update calls machine.Tick(), which runs CheckTransitions() FIRST and CurrentState.Tick() second. So on update frame N the condition is evaluated against the Timer value that frame N-1 left behind, namely (N-1)/60.","`idle.Timer >= 2f` first holds when (N-1)/60 >= 2, i.e. N - 1 >= 120, so the transition fires on frame 121. idle therefore ticked on frames 1 through 120 — exactly 120 ticks, 2.000 s.","On frame 121 SetState(active) calls idle.Exit(), assigns CurrentState = active and calls active.Enter() (Timer = 0); CheckTransitions then returns immediately. Tick() carries on and calls active.Tick(), so active already logs 1/60 s on the very frame it was entered, and idle.Tick() does not run on frame 121 at all.","Repeating the arithmetic for active: it ticks on frames 121..240, its condition first holds on frame 241, so the flip period is 241 - 121 = 120 frames = 2.000 s."],"result":"Each state runs for exactly 120 Tick() calls (2.000 s at 60 fps) and the period does not drift, because Enter() re-zeroes the timer and the incoming state ticks on its own entry frame. The only asymmetry is the entry frame itself: a state's last Tick() is the frame before the switch, never the switch frame.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Enemy AI with Idle / Patrol / Chase / Attack modes, each owning its entry and exit work.","Racing or vehicle AI, as in RacingAIController, whose Racing / Recovering / Finished states are thin wrappers calling back into the controller.","Round or match flow — Lobby, Countdown, Playing, Results — where each phase swaps UI and input bindings.","Character locomotion modes (grounded, airborne, climbing) that need different physics settings applied on entry.","Driving a graph authored in the Visual State Machine editor: VisualStateMachineRunner builds exactly this class from the asset, mapping 'Any State' nodes to null-from transitions."],"usage":{"code":"machine = new StateMachine();\nmachine.AddTransition(null, dead, () => health <= 0f);   // null 'from' = any state\nmachine.AddTransition(idle, chase, () => Vector3.Distance(transform.position, target.position) < 8f);\nmachine.SetState(idle);\n\nprivate void Update() => machine.Tick();","codeNote":"AddTransition is optional. Skip it entirely and call machine.SetState(...) from your own game logic if you would rather not use rule-based transitions — the source comment says as much."},"apiNotes":{"CurrentState":"The active IState, or null before the first SetState. Publicly readable, privately written. Tick() and SetState both use the null-conditional operator, so a machine with no state set is safe to pump.","SetState(IState)":"Returns immediately if newState is reference-equal to CurrentState; otherwise Exit() on the old state, assign, Enter() on the new. Because both calls are null-conditional, SetState(null) is a legal way to park the machine — it exits the current state and ticks nothing thereafter.","Tick()":"The pump. Evaluates transitions and then ticks the current state, in that order. Call it once per frame from the owning MonoBehaviour's Update; nothing calls it for you, and there is no internal frame counter or delta.","AddTransition(IState, IState, Func<bool>)":"Appends a rule to an ordered list; there is no remove, clear, or duplicate check. A null 'from' means the rule is evaluated no matter which state is current (a global 'any state -> Dead'). CheckTransitions skips rules whose 'from' is neither null nor the current state, so conditions on irrelevant rules are not even invoked.","IsInState<T>()":"Type test on the current state — returns true when the active state is a T. Use it for read-only queries (drive an animation, gate input); prefer a transition rule over branching on this."},"gotchas":[{"title":"First match wins, and the check loop returns","body":"CheckTransitions walks the list in registration order and does `SetState(to); return;` on the first condition that is true — so registration order is priority order, and one rule at most fires per Tick. A broad any-state rule registered early shadows everything after it while its condition holds, which is usually exactly what you want for 'any state -> Dead' and exactly what you do not want for a permissive catch-all."},{"title":"A self-transition is refused rather than accepted","body":"A rule whose from and to were the same state wedged the machine: the check found it, called SetState, which returned immediately because that state was already current, and then returned out of the loop — blocking every later rule for as long as the condition held. AddTransition now rejects it with an error, and an any-state rule pointing at the current state is skipped rather than ending the scan."},{"title":"SetState cannot re-enter the current state","body":"The guard `if (CurrentState == newState) return;` means you cannot restart a state by re-selecting it — Enter() will not run again. It is a reference comparison, so it also means each state must be a single long-lived instance: `SetState(new IdleState(this))` in Update never matches, and fires Exit/Enter every frame."},{"title":"A state can be entered and exited without ever ticking","body":"Transitions are checked before the state body runs, so a condition that is already true on the next Tick takes you straight back out. Enter() and Exit() are guaranteed to pair up; Tick() running at least once is not guaranteed."},{"title":"Transitions can never be removed","body":"There is no RemoveTransition or Clear, and AddTransition does not dedupe. Re-running your setup method — on OnEnable, on a respawn, on a difficulty change — appends a whole second copy of every rule, and the duplicates keep evaluating forever. Build a fresh StateMachine instead of re-registering on an existing one."},{"title":"Nothing pumps it and nothing tears it down","body":"It is not a MonoBehaviour. If the owning component is disabled, Tick() stops being called and the machine freezes mid-state with no Exit(); if the owner is destroyed, Exit() is never called at all. Anything a state acquires and must release needs a matching release in the owner's OnDisable/OnDestroy."},{"title":"Conditions run every frame and exceptions escape","body":"Every rule whose 'from' matches the current state has its Func<bool> invoked on each Tick, before any state logic. Keep them cheap and side-effect free — a physics query or a GetComponent in a condition runs at full frame rate. An exception thrown inside one propagates straight out of machine.Tick() and takes the rest of your Update with it."}],"seeAlso":["IState","VisualStateMachineRunner","RacingAIController"],"src_uses":["IState"],"src_usedBy":["BattleState","IState","RacingAIController","TurnManager","VisualStateMachineRunner"],"src_extensionPoints":["IState"],"isSystem":true,"systemOf":"StateMachine","systemPrimaryId":"state-machine","systemSections":[{"title":"Supporting scripts","entries":[{"id":"i-state","name":"IState","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"interface","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/StateMachine/IState.cs","src_lines":[4,9],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Implement one of these per state (Idle, Chase, Attack, etc.) and hand instances to a StateMachine.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Enter()","sig":"void Enter()","kind":"method","name":"Enter","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Tick()","sig":"void Tick()","kind":"method","name":"Tick","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Exit()","sig":"void Exit()","kind":"method","name":"Exit","static":false,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo","VisualStateMachine_Demo"],"entryKind":"contract","status":"published","summary":"Three-method contract (Enter/Tick/Exit) that StateMachine drives; write one class per state and hand it over.","purpose":"IState is the only thing StateMachine knows how to hold. It defines a state as three moments — the switch in, the per-frame body, the switch out — and nothing else: no context parameter, no deltaTime, no return value, no attributes, no base class. That minimalism is deliberate (StateMachine's own comment says it is kept framework-free so it is easy to trace and edit), and it is why anything can be a state: a private nested class, a plain POCO, or a MonoBehaviour that VisualStateMachineRunner binds to a graph node.","whenToImplement":"Write an IState when a behaviour has modes that are mutually exclusive, each with its own setup and teardown, and an enum plus a switch plus a pile of bools has started to hurt. The trigger is usually the third bool: an enemy that is not just chasing but chasing-unless-stunned-unless-dead, or a game flow where 'entering the round' and 'leaving the round' both need to do real work. If a mode has no entry or exit work at all, a plain enum is still the better answer.","memberContracts":{"Enter()":{"contract":"One-time setup for this activation: zero the counters this state owns, start the animation, enable the collider, pick a patrol target. StateMachine.SetState calls the outgoing state's Exit() first, then assigns CurrentState, then calls your Enter() — so by the time Enter runs, CurrentState already refers to you and IsInState<T>() already reports true.","mustNot":"Do not assume it will be called again when the machine re-selects the same state. SetState begins with `if (CurrentState == newState) return;`, so re-selecting the current state is a complete no-op — Enter() will not re-run, and you cannot use it to restart a state."},"Tick()":{"contract":"One frame of the state's behaviour, plus — if you are not using AddTransition — the decision to leave, by calling SetState on the machine yourself. StateMachine.Tick() evaluates transitions FIRST and then calls CurrentState.Tick(), so on a transition frame only the incoming state ticks; the outgoing state's last Tick() was the previous frame. There is no deltaTime argument: read Time.deltaTime (or the unscaled variant) yourself, and remember this runs at whatever rate the owner chooses to pump the machine.","mustNot":"Do not assume at least one Tick() happens between Enter() and Exit(). If a transition condition is already true on the next machine Tick, the state is entered and exited with zero Tick() calls in between, so never put required work here that Enter() should have done."},"Exit()":{"contract":"Undo exactly what Enter() did — stop the coroutine you started, disable the collider, clear the nav path, reset the counter for next time. It is called from SetState BEFORE CurrentState is reassigned, so CurrentState still refers to you while Exit() runs.","mustNot":"Do not treat this as guaranteed teardown. StateMachine has no Dispose, Stop, or destructor: if the owning MonoBehaviour is disabled or destroyed while this state is current, nothing ever calls Exit(). Anything that must be released (a subscription, a spawned effect, a disabled input map) needs a matching release in the OnDisable/OnDestroy of the owner as well."}},"minimalImpl":{"code":"public class ChaseState : IState\n{\n    private readonly EnemyAI owner;\n    public ChaseState(EnemyAI owner) => this.owner = owner;\n\n    public void Enter() => owner.PlayAnimation(\"Run\");\n    public void Tick()  => owner.MoveToward(owner.TargetPosition);\n    public void Exit()  => owner.StopMoving();\n}","note":"Constructor injection of the owner is the pattern every implementation in this toolkit uses — RacingAIController's RacingState/RecoveringState/FinishedState are private nested classes that hold an owner and do nothing but call back into it, keeping the real driving logic in one readable place instead of scattered across state files."},"wiring":{"code":"machine = new StateMachine();\nvar idle  = new IdleState(this);\nvar chase = new ChaseState(this);\nvar dead  = new DeadState(this);\n\nmachine.AddTransition(null, dead, () => health <= 0f);          // null 'from' = any state\nmachine.AddTransition(idle, chase, () => DistanceToTarget() < 8f);\nmachine.SetState(idle);                                          // calls idle.Enter()\n\nprivate void Update() => machine.Tick();   // transitions first, then CurrentState.Tick()","note":"StateMachine stores states by reference and compares with ==, so construct each state once and keep the reference. A transition whose 'from' is null is checked no matter which state is current, and CheckTransitions walks the list in registration order and returns on the first true condition — which is why registering the any-state 'dead' rule first is what gives it priority over everything else."},"extendGuide":"Almost everything you would want to vary lives in your own class, not in the interface. Per-state data: add public fields and let the transition lambdas read them — the Core demo does exactly this with machine.AddTransition(idle, active, () => idle.Timer >= 2f), where Timer is a public field that IdleState.Enter() zeroes and IdleState.Tick() accumulates. Shared context: take it in the constructor (the owner MonoBehaviour, a blackboard object, a config asset) rather than reaching for a singleton, or the state stops being reusable and testable. States that need Unity's Inspector: implement IState on a MonoBehaviour instead of a POCO — that is exactly what VisualStateMachineRunner expects, since it resolves each graph node to a bound component and logs a warning if that component does not implement IState. To add a genuinely new lifecycle hook (a FixedTick, say) you must edit two files — add the member to Core/StateMachine/IState.cs and a matching pump in Core/StateMachine/StateMachine.cs that calls CurrentState?.FixedTick() — and every existing implementation will then fail to compile until updated; with a three-member interface that is the intended trade-off. If only some states need it, prefer a second narrow interface plus a `if (CurrentState is IFixedTickState f) f.FixedTick();` check in the pump, which breaks nothing.","useCases":["Enemy AI modes — Idle, Patrol, Chase, Attack, Flee — where each mode has real setup (pick a patrol point, enable a weapon collider) and teardown (clear the nav path).","Game flow phases: Lobby, Countdown, Playing, RoundOver, each owning its own UI and input bindings.","Character locomotion modes where Grounded, Airborne, Climbing and Swimming need different physics settings applied on entry and restored on exit.","A node in a VisualStateMachineRunner graph: author the transitions visually, then bind a component implementing IState to supply that node's behaviour."],"gotchas":[{"title":"No context is passed in — take it in the constructor","body":"Enter/Tick/Exit take no arguments and return nothing, so a state has no way to be told who it belongs to unless you give it one. Every implementation in this toolkit holds a readonly owner supplied at construction. States that instead reach for singletons or FindObjectOfType compile fine and then break the moment you want two agents in the scene."},{"title":"States are instances, compared by reference","body":"SetState's guard is CurrentState == newState, a reference comparison. machine.SetState(new IdleState(this)) inside Update therefore never matches the current state, so Exit() and Enter() fire every single frame and any Enter-time reset is applied continuously. Construct each state once, store it in a field, and pass that same reference forever."},{"title":"Tick() gets no deltaTime and no guaranteed cadence","body":"It runs once per call to StateMachine.Tick(), which is however often the owner chooses to call it — typically Update, but nothing stops FixedUpdate or a coroutine. Read Time.deltaTime inside the state, and do not assume frame-rate independence you have not written."},{"title":"A MonoBehaviour state still has its own Update","body":"Implementing IState on a component (required by VisualStateMachineRunner) is supported, but Unity keeps calling that component's own Update, OnEnable and so on independently of the machine. Put the per-frame logic in Tick() only, or it runs twice — and once while the state is not even current."}],"calledBy":"StateMachine, and only StateMachine. Enter() and Exit() are called from SetState(); Tick() is called from StateMachine.Tick() AFTER transitions have been evaluated, which is why on a switch frame only the incoming state ticks. Nothing drives your methods on a schedule of its own — the machine runs exactly as often as its owner pumps it, usually once per Update, but nothing enforces that. VisualStateMachineRunner is the one indirect caller: it resolves each graph node to a bound component and drives it through the same StateMachine, so the ordering guarantees above hold there too.","drivenBy":["StateMachine","VisualStateMachineRunner"],"seeAlso":["StateMachine","VisualStateMachineRunner","RacingAIController"],"src_uses":["StateMachine"],"src_usedBy":["RacingAIController","StateMachine","VisualStateMachineRunner"],"src_extensionPoints":[]}]}]},{"id":"countdown-timer","name":"CountdownTimer","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/Timer/CountdownTimer.cs","src_lines":[7,63],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Plain timer driven manually via Tick(deltaTime) — works the same for a UI cooldown display, an ability cooldown, or a simple \"wait N seconds\" without spawning a coroutine.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Duration","sig":"public float Duration { get; private set; }","kind":"property","name":"Duration","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"TimeRemaining","sig":"public float TimeRemaining { get; private set; }","kind":"property","name":"TimeRemaining","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"IsRunning","sig":"public bool IsRunning { get; private set; }","kind":"property","name":"IsRunning","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Progress","sig":"public float Progress => Duration <= 0f ? 1f : 1f - (TimeRemaining / Duration)","kind":"property","name":"Progress","static":false,"comment":"0 = just started, 1 = finished. Feed this straight into a UI fill-bar Image."},{"id":"OnStarted","sig":"public event Action OnStarted","kind":"event","name":"OnStarted","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"OnCompleted","sig":"public event Action OnCompleted","kind":"event","name":"OnCompleted","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"CountdownTimer(float)","sig":"public CountdownTimer(float duration)","kind":"ctor","name":"CountdownTimer","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Stop()","sig":"public void Stop()","kind":"method","name":"Stop","static":false,"comment":"Pauses without resetting TimeRemaining — use Resume() to continue, or Start() to restart from Duration."},{"id":"Resume()","sig":"public void Resume()","kind":"method","name":"Resume","static":false,"comment":"Continues from wherever TimeRemaining was left off after Stop(). No-op if there's no time left."},{"id":"Tick(float)","sig":"public void Tick(float deltaTime)","kind":"method","name":"Tick","static":false,"comment":"Call this once per frame (e.g. from Update) with Time.deltaTime."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Plain C# countdown you tick yourself; exposes TimeRemaining, a 0-1 Progress, and start/complete events.","problem":"Unity gives you coroutines and Invoke for \"do this in N seconds\", but neither hands you the remaining time, neither is easy to pause and resume, and each one spawns machinery you then have to cancel. CountdownTimer is a ~60-line object with no Unity dependency at all: you construct it, call Tick(deltaTime) from your own Update, and read TimeRemaining or Progress whenever you want to draw a bar. Because you supply the delta, you decide whether the timer obeys Time.timeScale (pass Time.deltaTime) or ignores a pause (pass Time.unscaledDeltaTime).","workedExample":{"setup":"A 3-second ability cooldown driven from Update at a fixed 0.5 s step, then one longer step that overshoots zero.","inputs":[{"name":"duration","value":"3f"},{"name":"deltaTime per Tick","value":"0.5f (x5), then 0.75f"}],"steps":["new CountdownTimer(3f) sets Duration = 3 but leaves TimeRemaining at 0 and IsRunning false, so Progress reads 1 - (0 / 3) = 1 before the timer has ever run.","timer.Start() assigns TimeRemaining = Duration = 3, sets IsRunning = true and fires OnStarted. Progress = 1 - (3 / 3) = 0.","Five calls to timer.Tick(0.5f) subtract 2.5 total: TimeRemaining = 3 - 2.5 = 0.5, and Progress = 1 - (0.5 / 3) = 0.8333.","timer.Tick(0.75f) computes TimeRemaining = 0.5 - 0.75 = -0.25. Because that is <= 0 it is clamped back to exactly 0, IsRunning flips to false, and OnCompleted fires once from inside Tick.","Progress is now 1 - (0 / 3) = 1. The 0.25 s of overshoot is discarded, not carried into the next Start()."],"result":"After 3.25 s of supplied delta the timer reports TimeRemaining 0, IsRunning false and Progress 1, having fired OnCompleted exactly once. Restarting from that handler loses up to one frame of time per cycle.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["An ability or weapon cooldown whose remaining time drives a radial fill Image (Progress maps straight onto Image.fillAmount).","A round or match clock that must pause cleanly when the player opens a menu — Stop() freezes it, Resume() picks up mid-second.","A respawn or revive delay, where you also want to display \"2.4s\" rather than just be told when it is over.","Reusable per-enemy attack windup timers: one instance per agent, restarted with Start(newDuration) as the attack varies.","Anywhere you would otherwise write StartCoroutine + a bool flag + a cancellation check."],"usage":{"code":"private readonly CountdownTimer cooldown = new CountdownTimer(3f);\n\nprivate void Start()  { cooldown.OnCompleted += () => ready = true; cooldown.Start(); }\nprivate void Update() { cooldown.Tick(Time.deltaTime); fillBar.fillAmount = cooldown.Progress; }","codeNote":"Start() and Start(float newDuration) are real public methods on the class even though the member table below omits them (an extractor gap, not a missing API). The float overload reassigns Duration, so one instance can serve a different cooldown length each time."},"apiNotes":{"Duration":"The full length the timer was constructed with, or last restarted with via Start(newDuration). Only ever written by the constructor and that overload — Tick never touches it, which is what makes Progress a stable 0-1 ratio.","TimeRemaining":"Counts down in whatever units you feed Tick. Clamped to exactly 0 on completion, so it never goes negative and you can compare it to 0 safely.","IsRunning":"False before the first Start(), false after Stop(), and false the instant TimeRemaining hits 0. Tick() returns immediately when it is false, so a finished or paused timer costs nothing.","Progress":"1 - (TimeRemaining / Duration), guarded so a Duration of 0 or less reports 1 instead of dividing by zero. 0 at the moment of Start, 1 at completion — note it also reads 1 on a freshly constructed timer that has never started.","OnStarted":"Fires at the end of Start(), after TimeRemaining and IsRunning have already been set, so a handler can read the fresh state. There is no unsubscribe helper; you hold the -= yourself.","OnCompleted":"Fires from inside Tick(), after TimeRemaining is clamped to 0 and IsRunning is set false. Calling Start() from this handler is the supported way to loop (the demo runner does exactly that).","Stop()":"A pause, not a reset: it only clears IsRunning and deliberately leaves TimeRemaining where it was. Pair with Resume() to continue, or Start() to go back to the full Duration.","Resume()":"Sets IsRunning true only if TimeRemaining > 0. After a completed timer this is a no-op — an intentional guard so a stray Resume() cannot un-finish a timer that already fired OnCompleted.","Tick(float)":"The entire engine of the class. Does nothing unless IsRunning, subtracts the delta, and on crossing zero clamps, stops and raises OnCompleted in that order. Call it exactly once per frame per timer; calling it twice halves the effective duration."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Progress is 1 before the first Start()","body":"A freshly constructed timer has TimeRemaining = 0, and Progress is defined as 1 - TimeRemaining/Duration, so it reports 1 (\"finished\"). A fill bar bound to Progress in Awake shows completely full until something calls Start(). Either call Start() during setup or gate the UI on IsRunning."},{"title":"Resume() cannot restart a finished timer","body":"Resume() only re-arms when TimeRemaining > 0, and a completed timer sits at exactly 0. Calling Resume() there does nothing and fails silently. Use Start() (or Start(newDuration)) to run it again."},{"title":"Overshoot is thrown away, so loops drift","body":"When a Tick pushes TimeRemaining past zero, the excess is clamped off rather than carried over. A timer that restarts itself from OnCompleted therefore loses up to one frame per cycle — at 60 fps and a 1 s loop that is roughly 1.6% slow, and it accumulates. For anything that must stay in step with wall-clock time, track a target timestamp instead."},{"title":"Nothing ticks it for you","body":"This is not a MonoBehaviour and starts no coroutine. If the component that owns it is disabled, its Update stops and the timer simply freezes mid-count — usually what you want, but it means the timer is not a wall clock. It is also only as pause-aware as the delta you pass: Time.deltaTime honours Time.timeScale = 0, Time.unscaledDeltaTime ignores it."},{"title":"Event handlers stack on a reused instance","body":"There is no Reset or Clear. If you keep one timer for the lifetime of a component and subscribe to OnCompleted each time you Start() it, every restart adds another handler and the callback fires N times. Subscribe once, in Awake or Start."}],"seeAlso":["WaitForSecondsCache","CooldownAbilitySlot"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":["CooldownAbilitySlot","PlantWaterLevel"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"wait-for-seconds-cache","name":"WaitForSecondsCache","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/Coroutines/WaitForSecondsCache.cs","src_lines":[8,21],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"\"new WaitForSeconds(x)\" inside a loop/coroutine allocates every call; this reuses one instance per distinct duration. Usage: yield return WaitForSecondsCache.Get(0.5f);  instead of  yield return new WaitForSeconds(0.5f);","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Get(float)","sig":"public static WaitForSeconds Get(float seconds)","kind":"method","name":"Get","static":true,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Static dictionary of WaitForSeconds keyed by duration, so repeated coroutine yields stop allocating.","problem":"`yield return new WaitForSeconds(0.5f)` inside a loop allocates a fresh object on every single iteration, and every one of them is garbage a moment later. Multiply that by a long-running loop or a few dozen agents and it becomes a steady drip into the GC for no benefit whatsoever, because a WaitForSeconds carries nothing but its duration — Unity's coroutine scheduler tracks each coroutine's own wake time separately, which is exactly why one instance can safely be shared by any number of concurrent coroutines. This class caches one instance per distinct duration and hands the same object back forever.","workedExample":{"setup":"WaitForSecondsCacheDemoRunner's loop — `yield return WaitForSecondsCache.Get(0.5f);` inside a while(true) — left running for 60 seconds.","inputs":[{"name":"interval","value":"0.5 s"},{"name":"run time","value":"60 s"}],"steps":["60 / 0.5 = 120 loop iterations, so 120 yields.","Written as `yield return new WaitForSeconds(0.5f)`, that is 120 WaitForSeconds allocations, each one garbage as soon as the yield resumes.","Through the cache, the first Get(0.5f) misses the dictionary, allocates one WaitForSeconds and stores it under the key 0.5f; the remaining 119 calls are dictionary hits returning that same instance.","Allocations drop from 120 to 1: 1 - (1/120) = 0.9917, about a 99.2% reduction, and the dictionary holds exactly one entry.","Scale to 50 agents each running the same loop: 6,000 allocations become still 1, because the cache is static and keyed only by duration — not by caller, coroutine, or scene."],"result":"One WaitForSeconds object exists per distinct duration for the lifetime of the process, no matter how many coroutines yield on it or for how long. The saving is in allocation count and GC pressure, not in per-call speed.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["A spawner, turret, or damage-over-time tick that loops on a fixed interval for the whole level.","Per-agent AI coroutines — re-path every 0.25 s, re-scan for targets every 0.5 s — where the same few intervals repeat across dozens of instances.","UI polling or animation stagger loops that run continuously while a screen is open.","Anywhere a profiler capture shows a steady trickle of small GC allocations traced to coroutine yields."],"usage":{"code":"private IEnumerator FireLoop()\n{\n    while (true)\n    {\n        Shoot();\n        yield return WaitForSecondsCache.Get(0.5f);\n    }\n}","codeNote":"Drop-in: the returned object is an ordinary WaitForSeconds and behaves identically to one you constructed, including respecting Time.timeScale."},"apiNotes":{"Get(float)":"A TryGetValue on a static Dictionary<float, WaitForSeconds>, constructing and storing on a miss. Returns the same shared instance for every call with the same float value, forever — entries are never evicted and there is no clear method."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Only exactly equal float values share an entry","body":"The dictionary key is the raw float. Literal durations (0.5f, 1f, 0.25f) collapse to a handful of entries as intended, but a computed duration that differs in the last bit gets its own entry. The pathological case is a value that varies every call — Get(Time.deltaTime), Get(baseDelay * Random.value) — which allocates a new WaitForSeconds on every call anyway AND grows the dictionary without bound, turning the optimisation into a leak. Use this only for a small, fixed set of durations."},{"title":"The cache is static and never cleared","body":"Entries live for the lifetime of the process: across scene loads, and (in the Editor with Domain Reload disabled) across Play sessions. That is harmless for a dozen fixed durations and a slow leak for generated ones, and there is no API to flush it — you would have to add one."},{"title":"No unscaled equivalent, and do not try to cache one","body":"WaitForSeconds obeys Time.timeScale, so a cached wait freezes during a timeScale = 0 pause. The pause-proof alternative is WaitForSecondsRealtime, which must NOT be cached or shared: it is a CustomYieldInstruction that stores its own target wake time per instance, so two coroutines sharing one would corrupt each other's timing. Construct those fresh each time."},{"title":"Main thread only","body":"It is a plain Dictionary with no locking. Concurrent writes from another thread can corrupt it. In practice coroutines only run on the main thread, so this only bites if you call Get from a Task or a job."},{"title":"It saves allocations, not time","body":"A WaitForSeconds is a tiny object and the dictionary lookup is not free. This is worth doing in loops that repeat many times or across many agents; swapping it into a one-shot Start() coroutine buys nothing and just makes the line longer."}],"seeAlso":["CountdownTimer"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"extension-methods","name":"ExtensionMethods","cat":"Core","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.Core","src_file":"Core/Extensions/ExtensionMethods.cs","src_lines":[6,77],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"SetX(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetX(this Transform t, float x) { var p = t.position; p.x = x; t.position = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetX","static":true,"comment":"Transform position swizzle setters, saves writing \"var p = t.position; p.x = ...; t.position = p;\" everywhere."},{"id":"SetY(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetY(this Transform t, float y) { var p = t.position; p.y = y; t.position = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetY","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"SetZ(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetZ(this Transform t, float z) { var p = t.position; p.z = z; t.position = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetZ","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"SetLocalX(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetLocalX(this Transform t, float x) { var p = t.localPosition; p.x = x; t.localPosition = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetLocalX","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"SetLocalY(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetLocalY(this Transform t, float y) { var p = t.localPosition; p.y = y; t.localPosition = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetLocalY","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"SetLocalZ(this Transform, float)","sig":"public static void SetLocalZ(this Transform t, float z) { var p = t.localPosition; p.z = z; t.localPosition = p; }","kind":"method","name":"SetLocalZ","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"WithX(this Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 WithX(this Vector3 v, float x) => new Vector3(x, v.y, v.z)","kind":"method","name":"WithX","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"WithY(this Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 WithY(this Vector3 v, float y) => new Vector3(v.x, y, v.z)","kind":"method","name":"WithY","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"WithZ(this Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 WithZ(this Vector3 v, float z) => new Vector3(v.x, v.y, z)","kind":"method","name":"WithZ","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"WithX(this Vector2, float)","sig":"public static Vector2 WithX(this Vector2 v, float x) => new Vector2(x, v.y)","kind":"method","name":"WithX","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"WithY(this Vector2, float)","sig":"public static Vector2 WithY(this Vector2 v, float y) => new Vector2(v.x, y)","kind":"method","name":"WithY","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"DestroyChildren(this Transform)","sig":"public static void DestroyChildren(this Transform t)","kind":"method","name":"DestroyChildren","static":true,"comment":"Destroys every child under a transform, e.g. clearing a spawned-items container before repopulating it. The naive version — just calling Object.Destroy on each child — reads as if the container is empty afterwards, but Unity DEFERS Destroy to the end of the frame: childCount is unchanged on the very next line, and a container you clear and refill in the same Update ends up holding both sets. Each child is therefore DETACHED first, which takes effect immediately, so the container really is empty as soon as this returns; the orphaned objects are then destroyed on Unity's normal schedule. Outside Play mode Destroy never runs at all, so DestroyImmediate is used there instead."},{"id":"GetOrAddComponent<T>(this GameObject)","sig":"public static T GetOrAddComponent<T>(this GameObject go) where T : Component","kind":"method","name":"GetOrAddComponent","static":true,"comment":"Returns the existing component if present, otherwise adds and returns a new one — replaces the \"GetComponent, check null, AddComponent if needed\" boilerplate that comes up constantly."},{"id":"SetLayerRecursively(this GameObject, int)","sig":"public static void SetLayerRecursively(this GameObject go, int layer)","kind":"method","name":"SetLayerRecursively","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"Shuffle<T>(this IList<T>)","sig":"public static void Shuffle<T>(this IList<T> list)","kind":"method","name":"Shuffle","static":true,"comment":"Fisher-Yates shuffle in place, avoids the common (and statistically biased) \"sort by random key\" mistake."},{"id":"RandomItem<T>(this IList<T>)","sig":"public static T RandomItem<T>(this IList<T> list)","kind":"method","name":"RandomItem","static":true,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["CoreUtilities_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Static extension grab-bag: Transform/Vector component setters, DestroyChildren, recursive layer set, list shuffle.","problem":"Four or five snippets come up constantly in Unity code and are each annoying enough to get copy-pasted wrong: you cannot assign transform.position.x directly, you cannot replace one component of a Vector3 without rebuilding it, clearing a container means a reverse loop, and \"shuffle this list\" is usually written as a biased sort-by-random-key. This file collects the correct one-liners in one static class so the boilerplate disappears from call sites. It is pure convenience — nothing here has state, an Inspector presence, or a dependency on the rest of the toolkit.","workedExample":{"setup":"Shuffle<T>() on a 5-element List<int>, checking that the Fisher-Yates loop really is unbiased (the reason the source comment gives for not using a random-key sort).","inputs":[{"name":"list.Count","value":"5"}],"steps":["The loop runs from i = Count - 1 = 4 downward while i > 0, so it executes for i = 4, 3, 2, 1 and stops — 4 swaps. Index 0 is never a loop subject on its own because it is already reachable as a swap partner.","At each step the partner is Random.Range(0, i + 1), whose int overload is exclusive of the upper bound, giving i + 1 choices: 5 at i = 4, then 4, then 3, then 2.","Distinct swap sequences = 5 x 4 x 3 x 2 = 120.","A 5-element list has 5! = 120 orderings, and each distinct sequence lands on a different one, so the map is one-to-one: every ordering has probability exactly 1/120.","Contrast the mistake the source comment calls out — sorting with a random comparator, e.g. list.Sort((a, b) => Random.Range(-1, 2)) — which breaks the comparer's transitivity contract and yields an ordering that is neither uniform nor even well-defined across runtimes."],"result":"4 random draws, zero allocations, and a provably uniform permutation; the swap uses a C# tuple assignment so no temporary is needed.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Snapping an object to a ground height or a lane without clobbering its other axes: transform.SetY(hit.point.y).","Flattening a vector for planar movement or look-at maths: velocity.WithY(0f).","Clearing a dynamically populated UI container (inventory grid, scroll list) before repopulating it.","Moving a whole spawned prefab hierarchy onto a layer — ragdolls onto 'Corpse', a held weapon onto 'FirstPerson'.","Randomising a deck, a spawn table, or a set of quiz answers with a shuffle that is actually uniform."],"usage":{"code":"transform.SetY(hit.point.y);                 // world-space single-axis write\nVector3 flat = velocity.WithY(0f);           // copy with one component replaced\ncontainer.DestroyChildren();                 // clear a spawned-items container\ngameObject.SetLayerRecursively(corpseLayer); // whole hierarchy, no exceptions\ndeck.Shuffle();                              // unbiased Fisher-Yates, in place","codeNote":"Three more extensions exist in the file but are missing from the member table below because the extractor skips generic methods: GetOrAddComponent<T>(this GameObject) (TryGetComponent, else AddComponent), Shuffle<T>(this IList<T>), and RandomItem<T>(this IList<T>). They are real public API on this class."},"apiNotes":{"SetX(this Transform, float)":"Reads transform.position into a local, overwrites one component, writes it back. World space. Each call is a full Transform read plus write, so chaining SetX/SetY/SetZ costs three round-trips where one position assignment would do.","SetY(this Transform, float)":"As SetX, on the Y axis. The most-used of the set — ground snapping, lane switching, height clamping.","SetZ(this Transform, float)":"As SetX, on the Z axis.","SetLocalX(this Transform, float)":"The localPosition twin of SetX. Use these when the object is parented and you mean 'offset from my parent', not 'world coordinate'.","SetLocalY(this Transform, float)":"localPosition variant of SetY.","SetLocalZ(this Transform, float)":"localPosition variant of SetZ.","WithX(this Vector3, float)":"Returns a NEW Vector3 with X replaced — it does not mutate the value you called it on, because Vector3 is a struct passed by value. A bare `v.WithX(0f);` statement does nothing; you must assign the result.","WithY(this Vector3, float)":"Returns a copy with Y replaced. The idiomatic way to flatten a direction or velocity before normalising it for planar movement.","WithZ(this Vector3, float)":"Returns a copy with Z replaced.","WithX(this Vector2, float)":"Vector2 overload; the compiler picks it from the receiver type, so sharing a name with the Vector3 version is harmless.","WithY(this Vector2, float)":"Vector2 overload. There is no WithZ for Vector2, as expected.","DestroyChildren(this Transform)":"Iterates children backwards (childCount - 1 down to 0), detaches each one with SetParent(null, false), then calls Object.Destroy on it. Backwards is the safe direction for index-based child removal; the detach is what makes the container genuinely empty as soon as the call returns, since Destroy itself is deferred. Outside Play mode it uses DestroyImmediate, so the same call works from editor code.","SetLayerRecursively(this GameObject, int)":"Sets go.layer, then recurses into every direct child through the same extension, so the whole subtree ends up on one layer. Takes a layer INDEX, not a LayerMask: pass LayerMask.NameToLayer(\"Corpse\"), not a mask value, or you will silently set a nonsense layer."},"gotchas":[{"title":"DestroyChildren empties the container immediately, but the objects die at end of frame","body":"Object.Destroy is deferred by Unity, so the naive version of this method left childCount unchanged on the very next line — which silently doubles a container you clear and refill in the same Update. Each child is therefore DETACHED first, which takes effect at once: childCount reads 0 as soon as the call returns and a same-frame rebuild is safe. The orphaned objects still live until the end of the frame, so anything holding a reference to one, or counting them globally, sees them for one more frame. Outside Play mode the method uses DestroyImmediate instead, so it is also safe from an EditorWindow, a [MenuItem] or OnValidate."},{"title":"WithX/WithY/WithZ return a copy","body":"These take Vector3/Vector2 by value and return a new struct; unlike the Set* Transform extensions they mutate nothing. `transform.position.WithY(0f);` compiles and accomplishes absolutely nothing — you need `transform.position = transform.position.WithY(0f);` or, better, `transform.SetY(0f);`."},{"title":"SetLayerRecursively has no skip list","body":"It stomps every descendant unconditionally. Anything under that hierarchy that depends on its own layer — a hitbox on a damage layer, a UI element on a raycast layer, geometry a camera culls — silently changes behaviour. If you need exclusions, copy the four-line method and add the check rather than extending this one."},{"title":"RandomItem on an empty list returns default, not an error","body":"list.Count == 0 yields default(T): null for a class, 0 for an int, Vector3.zero for a struct. It never throws, so an empty spawn table looks like a legitimate result and the bug surfaces far from its cause. Check Count yourself wherever an empty list is possible."},{"title":"Shuffle and RandomItem use UnityEngine.Random","body":"The file imports UnityEngine and System.Collections.Generic but not System, so the unqualified Random is Unity's global generator, not System.Random. These draws share and advance the same seed as every other Unity random call, are reproducible via Random.InitState, and must not be called off the main thread."}],"seeAlso":["TweenExtensions"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]}],"groups":[{"title":"Lifetime & Instances","ids":["singleton","object-pool"]},{"title":"State","ids":["state-machine"]},{"title":"Time & Coroutines","ids":["countdown-timer","wait-for-seconds-cache"]},{"title":"Extensions","ids":["extension-methods"]}]};
